          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you. (Here explicitly: I love you as a sister.)\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The expression directly avows Jane's loving emotional state toward St. John, explicitly qualifying it as sisterly rather than marital love.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"as a sister, I love you\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"reason\":\"The love expression itself makes no promise or relational undertaking. Although Jane separately discusses accompanying St. John, she expressly denies being obligated and distinguishes sisterly love from becoming his wife; the separate offer should not be transferred onto the focal avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I am not under the slightest obligation to go to India\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I will be your curate, if you like, but never your wife\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The expression occurs within a reasoned explanation of why Jane would have ventured abroad with St. John. It is an assertive avowal, not a reflex-like or affectively discharged response.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"No core force beyond the avowal is needed. The phrase \\\"as a sister\\\" specifies the kind of love being presented as true rather than adding a distinct residual speech-act function.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"as a sister, I love you\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Jane addresses St. John during a dispute about whether she will go to India and whether she must marry him. She denies any obligation, rejects becoming his wife, and explains that her willingness to venture much with him arose from admiration, trust, and sisterly love. Thus the focal expression is a qualified avowal of non-marital affection in an argumentative clarification.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal expression undertakes nothing. Separately, Jane had offered to accompany St. John as his assistant or sister, while refusing marriage; St. John says an unmarried female curate would not suit him and proposes another missionary arrangement. Jane then denies any binding promise or obligation, so the terms and acceptance of the separate travel offer are contested and no uptake of it is established here.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognisable as Charlotte Brontë's Jane Eyre. The classification relies only on the supplied text.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage supports the sisterly-avowal reading and provides affirmative evidence of affection, but it cannot independently establish the full history, durability, or ultimate sincerity of Jane's feelings beyond this represented scene.\"}"
        }
